From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload routed IPv6 flows

Accept the IPv6 flows the flowtable offers with no address or port
rewrite (plain routing, both directions) and program them as ROUTE
entries of the IPv6 5-tuple flow table, keyed like the IPv4 NAT flows
(host entry = original source, flow address = destination), with the
nexthop supplying the egress port, the MAC rewrite and the tag inserts,
including the DSA user port encapsulation the WAN->LAN direction
needs. NAT66/NPTv6 (an IPv6 address or L4 port mangle) is declined, as
plain IPv4 routing still is.

The IPv6 entry is nine words and two slots of the table, the IPv6
host entry five words and two slots; the flow-table op takes the word
count, and the flow_owner[] bookkeeping (supersede on a hash-mode
overwrite, disown on destroy or a failed re-point) covers the slot
span of an entry. The op result index and the per-slot hit counter
are taken to be in slot units for IPv6 as they are for IPv4 (the
vendor SDK addresses the counter with the returned index for both
families); an IPv6 add landing at an odd index is reported once and
declined, since that would mean the unit differs. Should a hash-mode
add ever land over part of a live entry of the other width, the slots
of that entry outside the new one are written empty rather than left
programmed and untracked. The ingress L3 interface now enables IPv6
unicast routing next to IPv4; the hop-limit decrement follows the TTL
decrement already in force.

The software fast path sends fragments, and IPv6 packets with any
extension header, through the slow path; a hardware entry matches on
the 5-tuple the parser found and would forward the first fragment or
carry an extension header past software. The parser exceptions for
fragments of both families and for IPv6 extension headers (with the
malformed-header and small-hop-limit codes) are therefore programmed
at init to redirect such packets to the CPU on a flow hit, without
de-accelerating the flow, with the same read-back verification as the
TCP control-flag exceptions; IPv6 is declined if that fails.

Both address families must name their family in n_proto and carry
exact address masks, so a hand-written rule cannot reach the encoder
with a broader match. The IPv4 path had no such mask check before
this change: a rule with a non-exact IPv4 address match could have been
programmed as a host-exact entry, so this is a correction to the IPv4
path as well, not only IPv6 plumbing. An
IPv6 flow entering from anywhere but the switch is accepted only when
its source is one the kernel's own route reaches through that ingress
device: the rule for a PPPoE WAN's reply names the plain Ethernet
device and carries no trace of the session, and a plain-framed entry
would forward what software drops -- the counterpart of the IPv4 DNAT
address check.

Entry layouts follow the vendor SDK's struct in_flow_ipv6_5tuple_tbl_2
and struct host_ipv6_tbl (APPE branch) and its assignment code; the
128-bit address is packed as one big-endian integer, least significant
bits first, and the packing was checked against a model of the vendor's
for 20000 random addresses. The FDB follower re-points an IPv6 entry
like an IPv4 one.

Tested on a GL-BE9300 (2026-09-28/29): IPv6 download and upload 2321 Mbit/s
at 99% idle on the default rtl8_4 tagger, hardware both ways; wan.10 upload
in hardware, wan.10 download declined under the alias by design; IPv4
unchanged at 2354 Mbit/s; fragments 0% loss both families both directions;
hop-by-hop and hop-limit-1 segments reach the CPU and the flow stays in
hardware; 90 s liveness and 40-connection teardown clean; VLAN suite 4/4.
The host entry of a routed IPv6 flow is marked LAN whichever way it enters:
marked WAN (as first written) the WAN->LAN entry never hit (CPU code 163).
not yet run on hardware.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
